Your password has not been changed.

The password you gave is shorter than $_arg_limit characters, and fml
does not store one below that length.

The reason is particular to fml.  Before this version a password was
stored in a format that kept only its first eight characters and threw
the rest away, so every password an existing list holds is worth eight
characters whatever was typed to set it.  Accepting a new one of eight
or nine would leave you exactly where you started.

Please choose a password of fifteen characters or more.

Three documents, and what each of them asks for:

  NIST SP 800-63B asks for at least fifteen characters for a password
  used on its own, which an fml administrator password is.  It also
  says a verifier "SHALL NOT impose other composition rules (e.g.,
  requiring mixtures of different character types)", and must not
  require periodic changes.
  https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html

  NISC's "Internet Anzen Anshin Handbook" treats ten characters or more
  as the range in which a password is safe, and says periodic change is
  not needed.
  https://security-portal.cyber.go.jp/handbook/

  The Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications, on its
  cybersecurity site for the public, recommends "a reasonably long
  random sequence of letters and digits", and suggests joining several
  unrelated words with digits between them rather than making a short
  one awkward.
  https://www.soumu.go.jp/main_sosiki/cybersecurity/kokumin/security/business/staff/06/

Length is what all three are pointing at, not punctuation.  A phrase you
can remember serves better than a short string you cannot.
